Consider the triangular prism shown below, where

* the area of the base triangular face is [math]B,[/math] and

* the height (or length, depending on how we look at the prism) is [math]L[/math].

\documentclass{article}
\usepackage{amsmath}
\usepackage{pgfplots}
\usepackage{tikz}
\usepackage{tkz-euclide}
\usepackage{ifthen}
\usetkzobj{all}

\newcommand{\myTrigPyramid}[6]{
		\coordinate (B) at (#1,0,0);
		\coordinate (C) at (#1,0,-#2);
		\coordinate (D) at (0,0,-#2);
		\coordinate (B') at (#1,#3,0);
		\coordinate (C') at (#1,#3,-#2);
		\coordinate (D') at (0,#3,-#2);

		% Faces
		\tkzDrawPolygon[draw=none, fill=#4!15, fill opacity=#5](B,C,C',B')
		\tkzDrawPolygon[draw=none, fill=#4!15, fill opacity=#5](C,D,D',C')
		\tkzDrawPolygon[draw=none, fill=#4!15, fill opacity=#5](D,B,B',D')

		% Base
		\tkzDrawPolygon[draw=none, fill=#4!50, fill opacity=#5](B,C,D)
		%Top
		\tkzDrawPolygon[draw=none, fill=#4!15, fill opacity=#5](B',C',D')

		% Edges
		\tkzDrawSegments[thin, color=#4, dashed, opacity=0.75](C,D)
		\tkzDrawSegments[very thick, color=#4](B,C B,B' C,C' D,D' B,D B',C' C',D' D',B')
}

\begin{document}
\pagestyle{empty}

\definecolor{deepskyblue}{rgb}{0.173,0.447,0.682}
\definecolor{orangepeel}{rgb}{0.902, 0.624, 0}
\definecolor{lightyellow}{rgb}{0.953,0.882,0.388}
\definecolor{darkgreen}{rgb}{0.271, 0.608, 0.463}
\definecolor{lightblue}{rgb}{0.484,0.702,0.894}
\definecolor{rustred}{rgb}{0.784,0.392,0.149}
\definecolor{fadedpink}{rgb}{0.753,0.494,0.643}

%\tdplotsetmaincoords{60}{50}
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1,rounded corners=0.1mm, rotate=0]%tdplot_main_coords

	\myTrigPyramid{3.5}{6}{4}{deepskyblue}{1};
	\node[] at (3.5*3/4,0,-6*2/3) {[math]B[/math]};
	\node[right] at (3.5,4/2,-6) {[math]L[/math]};

\end{tikzpicture}
\end{document}

Then, the volume of the triangular prism is given by
\[
V = B \cdot L.
\]

For example, suppose [math]B=18\:\text{m}^2[/math] and [math]L=5\:\text{m}.[/math] Substituting these into the formula, we get
\[
V = 18 \cdot 5 = 90 \: \text{m}^3.
\]

Let's see some more examples.

