Now, consider the prism below.

\documentclass{article}
\usepackage{amsmath}
\usepackage{pgfplots}
\usepackage{tikz}
\usepackage{tkz-euclide}
\usetkzobj{all}

\newcommand{\myTrigPyramid}[7]{
	\coordinate (H) at (-#6,0,0);

	\coordinate (B) at (#1/2,0,0);
	\coordinate (C) at (-#1/2,0,0);
	\coordinate (D) at (-#6,#2,0);

	\coordinate (B') at (#1/2,0,-#3);
	\coordinate (C') at (-#1/2,0,-#3);
	\coordinate (D') at (-#6,#2,-#3);

	% Faces
	\tkzDrawPolygon[draw=none, fill=#4!15, fill opacity=#5](B,C,C',B')
	\tkzDrawPolygon[draw=none, fill=#4!15, fill opacity=#5](C,D,D',C')
	\tkzDrawPolygon[draw=none, fill=#4!15, fill opacity=#5](D,B,B',D')

	% Base
	\tkzDrawPolygon[draw=none, fill=#4!15, fill opacity=#5](B,C,D)
	%Top
	\tkzDrawPolygon[draw=none, fill=#4!15, fill opacity=#5](B',C',D')

	% Edges
	\tkzDrawSegments[thin, color=#4, dashed, opacity=0.75](C',D' C,C' B',C')
	\tkzDrawSegments[very thick, color=#4](B,C B,B' D,D' B,D C,D D',B')
	\tkzDrawSegments[dashed, thick, color=gray](D,H)

	\tkzLabelSegment[right](D,H){[math]5\:\text{m}[/math]}
	\tkzLabelSegment[below](B,C){[math]6\:\text{m}[/math]}
	\tkzLabelSegment[right=2pt](B,B'){[math]10\:\text{m}[/math]}
}

\begin{document}
\pagestyle{empty}

\definecolor{lightblue}{rgb}{0.484,0.702,0.894}

\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.6,rounded corners=0.1mm, rotate=0]

	\myTrigPyramid{6}{5}{10}{lightblue}{1}{1}{m};

\end{tikzpicture}
\end{document}

First of all, notice that it is still a triangular prism, just with a different orientation in space.
What is the volume of this prism?


As we have seen from the beginning of the lesson, the volume of a triangular prism is given by
\[
V = BL,
\]
where [math]B[/math] is the area of the base and [math]L[/math] is the length (height) of the prism. But this time, we are not given the area of the triangular face directly. Despite this, we can still compute the volume!

Now, if [math]b[/math] and [math]h[/math] are the base and the height of the base triangle of the prism, respectively, then the area of the triangle is
\[
B=\dfrac{1}{2}bh.
\]

Thus, the formula for the volume of the triangular prism becomes
\[
V = \dfrac{1}{2}bhL.
\]

Therefore, substituting 
\[
b=6\:\text{m}, \qquad
h=5\:\text{m}, \qquad
L=10\:\text{m}
\]
into the formula, we obtain
\[
V = \dfrac{1}{2} \cdot 6 \cdot 5 \cdot 10 = 150 \: \text{m}^3.
\]

