tutorial.content:
Triangular prisms naturally arise in many real-world problems involving volume.

For example, suppose a grain silo is shaped like a triangular prism, as shown below. Grain is poured into the silo at a rate of [math]2[/math] cubic meters per second. How long will it take to completely fill the silo?

\documentclass{article}
\usepackage{amsmath}
\usepackage{pgfplots}
\usepackage{tikz}
\usepackage{tkz-euclide}
\usetkzobj{all}

\newcommand{\myTrigPyramid}[7]{
	\coordinate (H) at (-#6,0,0);

	\coordinate (B) at (#1/2,0,0);
	\coordinate (C) at (-#1/2,0,0);
	\coordinate (D) at (-#6,#2,0);

	\coordinate (B') at (#1/2,0,-#3);
	\coordinate (C') at (-#1/2,0,-#3);
	\coordinate (D') at (-#6,#2,-#3);

	% Faces
	\tkzDrawPolygon[draw=none, fill=#4!15, fill opacity=#5](B,C,C',B')
	\tkzDrawPolygon[draw=none, fill=#4!15, fill opacity=#5](C,D,D',C')
	\tkzDrawPolygon[draw=none, fill=#4!15, fill opacity=#5](D,B,B',D')

	% Base
	\tkzDrawPolygon[draw=none, fill=#4!15, fill opacity=#5](B,C,D)
	%Top
	\tkzDrawPolygon[draw=none, fill=#4!15, fill opacity=#5](B',C',D')

	% Edges
	\tkzDrawSegments[thin, color=#4, dashed, opacity=0.75](C',D' C,C' B',C')
	\tkzDrawSegments[very thick, color=#4](B,C B,B' D,D' B,D C,D D',B')
	\tkzDrawSegments[dashed, thick, color=gray](D,H)

	\tkzLabelSegment[right](D,H){[math]7\:\text{m}[/math]}
	\tkzLabelSegment[below](B,C){[math]6\:\text{m}[/math]}
	\tkzLabelSegment[right=2pt](B,B'){[math]10\:\text{m}[/math]}
}

\begin{document}
\pagestyle{empty}

\definecolor{rustred}{rgb}{0.784,0.392,0.149}

\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.6,rounded corners=0.1mm]
	\myTrigPyramid{6}{7}{10}{rustred}{1}{1}{m};
\end{tikzpicture}

\end{document}

First, we find the volume. The volume of a triangular prism is given by
\[
V = BL,
\]
where [math]B[/math] is the area of the base and [math]L[/math] is the height (length) of the prism. 

Now, if [math]b[/math] and [math]h[/math] are the base and the height for the base triangle of the prism, respectively, then the area of the triangle is
\[
B=\dfrac{1}{2}bh.
\]

Thus, the formula for the volume of the triangular prism becomes
\[
V = \dfrac{1}{2}bhL.
\]

Therefore, substituting 
\[
b=6\:\text{m}, \qquad
h=7\:\text{m}, \qquad
L=10\:\text{m}
\]
into the formula, we obtain
\[
V = \dfrac{1}{2} \cdot 6 \cdot 7 \cdot 10 = 210 \: \text{m}^3.
\]

Now, we compute the total time needed to fill the silo. The filling rate is [math]2[/math] cubic meters per second, so we get 
\[
210 \div 2 = 105.
\]

Let's check the units:
\[
\require{cancel}
\text{m}^3 \div \dfrac{\text{m}^3}{\text{s}} = \cancel{\text{m}^3} \cdot \dfrac{\text{s}}{\cancel{\text{m}^3}} = \text{s} \:\: {\color{darkgreen}\checkmark}
\]

Therefore, it takes [math]105[/math] seconds to fill the silo.

