The volume of a triangular prism is given by
\[
V = BL,
\]
where [math]B[/math] is the area of the base and [math]L[/math] is the height (length) of the prism. 

Now, if [math]b[/math] and [math]h[/math] are the base and the height for the base triangle of the prism, respectively, then the area of the triangle is
\[
B=\dfrac{1}{2}bh.
\]
Thus, the formula for the volume of the triangular prism becomes
\[
V = \dfrac{1}{2}bhL.
\]

Therefore, substituting 
\[
b=3, \qquad
h=6, \qquad
V=54
\]
into the formula, we obtain the following equations, which we solve for [math]L{:}[/math]
\begin{align*}
\dfrac{1}{2} \cdot 3 \cdot 6 \cdot L &= 54\\[5pt]
9L &= 54 \\[5pt]
L &= \dfrac{54}{9} \\[5pt]
&= 6
\end{align*}

Therefore, the length of the triangular prism is [math]L=6\:\text{ft}.[/math]

